WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.070
The third rule is the token check,

2
00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:04.988
and it runs before a live
job makes a single call.

3
00:00:04.988 --> 00:00:09.000
An app-only token lists its
application permissions in one claim,

4
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.899
so the Python client reads
it from its own token.

5
00:00:11.899 --> 00:00:14.637
If a permission is missing, the run stops.

6
00:00:14.637 --> 00:00:17.400
If there's one the task
doesn't use, it warns,

7
00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:20.650
because an unused permission
is still exposure.

8
00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:23.684
PowerShell is trickier,
since the Graph SDK

9
00:00:23.684 --> 00:00:26.887
signs in for you and never
hands over the token.

10
00:00:26.887 --> 00:00:30.580
It turns out the session's context
reports the same permissions,

11
00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:33.980
read from the same claim, so
the module checks that instead.
